# Rust build output. The exec shim (`proc-shim/`) is its own crate with its own target dir;
# sbx's build script builds it under OUT_DIR, but a manual build there lands here.
/target
/proc-shim/target

# Backup files created manually (never commit)
*.bak
*.bak-*

# Per-contributor mise overlay. Lets you add local-only settings on top of the
# shared `mise.toml` without touching the committed file. Read automatically by mise.
mise.local.toml
.mise.local.toml

# Local flake experiments (never committed); contributors who want to try one
# locally drop flake.nix/flake.lock here and they stay untracked.
flake.nix
flake.lock

# Environment / secrets (never committed)
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Editor / IDE noise
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Docusaurus: node_modules + build artifacts (regenerated by `mise run docs-install` /
# `mise run docs-build`). Never commit.
docs-site/node_modules/
docs-site/build/
docs-site/.docusaurus/

# Scratch git worktrees an agent session creates under `.claude/` to work on an isolated copy of
# the tree. They are checkouts of this same repository, so a `git add -A` picks them up as embedded
# repositories rather than as files — commit one and the history gains a gitlink pointing at a path
# that will not exist. `.claude/settings.json` beside them is tracked and stays so.
.claude/worktrees/

# Runtime state a caged app writes into the project root. `freebuff` keys its per-project data by a
# `project-id` it drops here alongside a SQLite database, so the directory appears the first time
# `sbx app run freebuff` is used on this tree and is never something to commit. Listed for the same
# reason as the worktrees above: it is untracked residue at the root, where a `git add -A` sweeps it
# into a commit that has nothing to do with it.
.freebuff/
